# /etc/inittab — BusyBox init (M108).
#
# PID 1 on b1nix is /sbin/openrc-init by DEFAULT (kernel/main.c, M94). This
# file is read only when the kernel cmdline selects BusyBox init instead:
#
#     init=/opt/busybox/bin/init
#
# That is a deliberate choice, not an accident of ordering. OpenRC owns the
# service graph b1nix already ships (etc/init.d + etc/runlevels, M94), and
# openrc-init additionally provides the /run/openrc/init.ctl control channel
# that openrc-shutdown and telinit talk to. BusyBox init has no equivalent, so
# demoting OpenRC to a plain runlevel driver would lose a working feature.
# Keeping openrc-init as the default and reaching BusyBox init through `init=`
# means both are real, bootable configurations and neither is a fork of the
# other's service definitions.
#
# The reconciliation below is Alpine's: BusyBox init is the supervisor (it
# reaps orphans, runs the sysinit/shutdown phases and respawns getty), while
# OpenRC still drives every runlevel, so the same /etc/init.d services and the
# same /etc/local.d hooks run under either PID 1.
#
# Format:  <id>:<runlevels>:<action>:<process>   (id/runlevels unused by BusyBox)

# Bring the system up through OpenRC's own runlevels, in OpenRC's order.
::sysinit:/sbin/openrc sysinit
::sysinit:/sbin/openrc boot
::wait:/sbin/openrc default

# Test hook: only does anything when the kernel cmdline carries b1nix.test=1
# AND b1nix.smoke=bbinit (see the script). On an ordinary boot it exits at once.
::wait:/bin/sh /etc/bbinit-smoke.sh

# Interactive login on the serial console. b1nix's console is COM1
# (/dev/ttyS0); getty execs /bin/login, which is the setuid BusyBox copy.
::respawn:/bin/getty -L 115200 ttyS0 vt100

# Ctrl-Alt-Del and shutdown handling.
::ctrlaltdel:/sbin/reboot
::shutdown:/sbin/openrc shutdown
::restart:/opt/busybox/bin/init
